  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nWhile the jet stream is relatively mundane in terms of \ncurvature it will remain firmly overhead directing any \ntransient shortwave activity into the Dakotas and Minnesota over\nthe next several days. With moist southerly flow via the \ncentral plains into the northern plains corn advection pipeline \ndewpoints will easily climb into the 60s the next several days \nleading to somewhat humid but overall seasonable conditions. We \nwill be in the 70s to 80s most days with an outside shot 90 in \nthe central/southern valley Saturday. Aside from scattered \nthunderstorm chances the next several days look to be overall a \nrelatively dry stretch with no soaking rains on the horizon and \nno other eye catching hazard potential as RH stays above 35% and\nwind stays under 30 mph. Something could certainly pop up but \nright now the main threat for any hazardous weather stems from \nthunderstorm activity. \n\n- Saturday Night Storms\n\nA weak 500mb shortwave should be transiting northern Minnesota \nby 00z Sunday with minimal action expected from this as it \nremains displaced too far north from the moisture or \ninstability. A trailing inverted trough at 850mb will be the \narea of focus overnight for initiation with a nice plume of \ntheta-e advection ahead of it. There is however quite a \ndisparity in the placement of the moisture gradient and its \nnorthward extent which will also dictate the degree of \ninstability available. We could see a more bullish scenario like\nthe NAM where MUCAPE is in excess of 3000 J/kg or something on \nthe lower end like the RAP with only 1000-2000 J/kg where things\nstay more south of the area. Unlike instability the shear is \nmore broadly agreed upon with anywhere from 40-50kts (locally \nstronger scenarios exist). This combined with the fact elevated \nconvection seems to be the only possible evolution given a \nnocturnal inversion and elevated hailers seem to be the primary \nhazard. Given the environment (assuming storms form) would think\nhail anywhere from 1-1.5\" on the low end to up to 2.5-2.75\" is \npossible if something closer to the NAM verifies. We'll \ncertainly know more by this time tomorrow. Fro reference the \nhail we got out in Park Rapids this morning occurred in a very \nsimilar thermodynamic environment with markedly less shear if \nthat helps to paint the picture for Saturday.\n\n- Sunday and beyond\n\nAs the jet remains overhead no shortwaves are currently \nstanding out in numerical guidance but given 1000-2000 J/kg \nMUCAPE and Eff shear of 30-40kts most days its certainly \nplausible to expect numerous days to have storms, the severity \nof which along with their placement is highly uncertain. The \nfirst days that begin to stand out are Tuesday and Wednesday for\nthis potential but will wait until more guidance comes into \nbetter agreement to start increasing messaging.",
